    "body": "The California-based defense company Kratos announced this week the first test flight of the uncrewed combat aircraft known as the U740 Valkyrie over European skies, as well as the first successful flight tests of a new turbojet engine designed for small aerial systems.\nThe U740 Valkyrie was developed through a partnership with the Dutch company Airbus in support of German and other European armed forces.\nIn a joint statement , Mike Schoellhorn, the chief executive of Airbus Defence and Space, explained that the Valkyrie combined Kratos’ low-cost airframe with Airbus’ sovereign European mission system.\n“In an increasingly complex and contested threat environment, air forces require affordable combat mass today,” he said, adding that the Valkyrie will also support crewed combat aircraft with extended range, firepower and survivability.\nThe companies said the test flight accomplished all of their goals, including an independent rocket-assisted takeoff, maneuvers at various altitudes, speeds up to Mach 0.8, and in-flight mission adjustments.\nAccording to the statement, the Valkyrie runs 30 feet in overall length with a 27-foot wingspan. It features an internal weapons bay for air-to-air and air-to-ground munitions. Additionally, it has a max take-off weight of more than 6,000 pounds, a range exceeding 3,000 miles and a service ceiling of 45,000 feet.\nThe companies added that the successful demonstration paves the way for more sophisticated test flights with Airbus’ Multi-platform Autonomous Reconfigurable and Secure mission system, or MARS.\nThey described MARS as the aircraft’s “European brain” because humans will still control all critical decisions despite the AI-enhanced software that allows the system to function autonomously.\nEric DeMarco, the president and CEO of Kratos, said that although “first flights typically include plenty of learning opportunities,” he called the test “an amazing success” because of the maturity of the technology.\nThe J85 Engine\nThis week, Kratos also announced recent flight tests of the TDI-J85 engine across multiple configurations, meaning it demonstrated capabilities for cruise missiles, decoys and unmanned aerial systems, or UAS.\n“Our successful flight tests across diverse deployment methods prove the versatility and reliability of the TDI-J85 engine,” said Joseph Kovasity, a senior vice president of Kratos.\nAccording to the statement , the J85 engine successfully launched from standard rail or zero-length launchers, an aircraft cargo bay and a launch container.\nKovasity added that Kratos recently scaled up operations because of manufacturing orders to build 3,000 engines in 2027, but the company is positioning itself to produce up to 10,000 engines or more per year by 2029.\nAbout Daniel Terrill\nDaniel Terrill is a contributor to Military Times. He’s been reporting on military issues, the gun industry, and the outdoors for nearly two decades. Although writing is his passion, he’s been a Marine, police officer, and, perhaps the most dangerous job of his career, a substitute teacher.\nShare:\nMore In MilTech\nTrust between operators, CCAs starts with simulations: Former Air Force secretary\nSimulations and exercises will be key to boosting trust between operators and CCAs, according to former U.S. Air Force secretary Frank Kendall.\nCelebrating 40 years of Defense News\nSince 1986, Defense News has been the authoritative voice for the world's defense decision-makers.\nGuardsman learns to fly autonomous Black Hawk in less than an hour\nIn a training first, an Army National Guardsman used a tablet to control Sikorsky's optionally piloted Black Hawk and plan its task during an exercise.\nRecap: Highlights from the US Army’s 2025 conference in Washington\nThe Army continues to transform, with efforts focused on next-gen weaponry, long-range artillery, unmanned systems and much more.\nPentagon stages first ‘Top Drone’ school for operators to hone skills\nIndustry and military drone operators flew tethered and untethered first-person drones through a course designed to test endurance and maneuverability.",
